 {"anchor": ("p", "Nevertheless, this study has several important limitations"), "pos": "after", "nodes": [
  ("p", "One assumption carries more weight than the others. The demand distribution is held fixed when designs are compared, yet a lot that becomes easier to park in attracts more attempts, and a rule that reserves charging berths may send charging EVs elsewhere. Rahimian and Mehrotra {M04} show how decision-dependent ambiguity changes both the model and its computational cost, and their result is a boundary here rather than a method: without records on refused attempts the counterfactual demand cannot be identified, and replaying historical demand under a new rule is an assumption about behaviour, not an estimate of it."),
  ("p", "The objective is expected social cost. A planner who has to meet a service target rather than minimise a mean can borrow the robust satisficing view {M02} and ask how far the distribution may move before an acceptable cost or service level is lost. Distributional robustness offers two implementations, a Wasserstein ball around the empirical distribution {P19} and a moment-based set {P20}, and both need assumptions our data do not obviously meet, notably light tails for the value-of-time distribution and a non-empty moment set. Robustness is therefore used as a stress test first: same mean, different shape, different correlation, different dwell distribution."),
  ("p", "If a prediction module is added, its value should be measured by the decision loss it induces rather than by arrival error {P17, P18}. The predict-then-optimise framework makes this concrete, and its applicability depends on how the predicted quantity enters the constraints. In our model the EV share enters the feasible set and not only the objective, so a decision-oriented loss would need rederivation rather than an off-the-shelf implementation."),
  ("p", "A static ratio is a planning object and cannot respond within a day. Rolling operation, in which open and protected berths are revised as vehicles arrive and leave, is the natural extension {P25}. Learning-augmented switching {P16}, Bayesian online selection {P15} and online allocation of reusable resources {P10} describe what a guarantee would look like if the arrival distribution were known and the state small enough; we do not claim such guarantees here. The same caution applies to the prediction lines not pursued in this paper. Self-exciting and neural point processes {P02, P03, P04} need per-vehicle event labels that the snapshots do not carry, robust dynamic admission {P21} needs transition probabilities that cannot be estimated from vacancy counts, and adaptive or non-exchangeable conformal intervals {P23, P24} describe coverage of forecasts, not of the configuration decision."),
 ]},
